////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// Multi-line transitive chain tests for the Peggy parsers
//
// Each blank-line-separated block below is parsed as a single input
// by BOTH parsers ('blocks' mode in parsertests.js).  Newline
// positions inside a chain are significant: the tex parser chooses
// an align* layout when a chain operator has a newline on either
// side, so these cannot be tested by the line-at-a-time suites.
// Full-line // comments (like this header) separate blocks and are
// not parsed; trailing // comments inside a block are chain syntax.

x = y
= z by algebra

x = y
leq z // comment
= w by arithmetic

x leq y
leq z
leq w by algebra

x = y
= z
= w by cases

// a chain can mix same-line and newline operators

x = y = z
= w by substitution

a+b leq c
= d by arithmetic

// an expository comment with no final by reason

x = y
= z // just a comment

x^2-1 = (x-1) cdot (x+1) // factor
= 0 by algebra

// congruence chains and mixed =/cong chains
// use the align* layout exactly like the inequality family

a cong_(2) b
cong_(2) c by test

5+7 = 12 by arithmetic
cong_(3) 0 // 12 is a multiple of 3

x cong_(m) y // given
= z
cong_(m) w by transitivity

// the |, ⊆, and bare-≅ families
// use the align* layout exactly like the inequality family

m | n by thm1
| p // divisibility is transitive
= q

A subset B // A is contained in B
subset C by transitivity

A = B by defn
subset C // and B is inside C

a cong b by SAS
cong c // congruence is transitive
= d by defn

// a bare operator mention may open a | or ⊆ chain across lines

~ subset A cross A // a relation on A
subset B cross B by monotonicity
